**1. The biggest difference: simplicity versus control**  
**Our prompt**  
Our current prompt tells the model:  
Read the Stage 1 files → understand the learning objective → create an appropriate real-world application → write 3 connected questions → verify the math → create a one-page PDF.  
That makes it relatively straightforward.  
**Hitaansh prompt**  
The other prompt introduces several intermediate systems before the case can even be generated:  
    •    calibration gate;  
    •    ground-truth hierarchy;  
    •    scope fence;  
    •    12 invariant principles;  
    •    P1–P8 derived playbook;  
    •    weak-mapping warning;  
    •    attribution tracking;  
    •    instructor alignment material;  
    •    compile verification;  
    •    verification comments embedded in LaTeX.  
That gives it more control and traceability but also creates much more instruction overhead.  
**My view:** for your actual MathGPT pipeline, our current architecture is better as the core production prompt. Some of Hitaansh's reasoning rules should be incorporated, but I would not adopt its entire structure.  
