**3. They treat learning objectives differently**  
This is an important philosophical difference.  
**Our current prompt**  
Our version says:  
    •    primary.md decides **what should be taught**;  
    •    primary.md decides **what should be taught**;  
    •    question.txt establishes the **skill and difficulty**;  
    •    question.txt establishes the **skill and difficulty**;  
    •    verified_answer.txt establishes **mathematical correctness and solution logic**.  
    •    verified_answer.txt establishes **mathematical correctness and solution logic**.  
I think this division is strong.  
**Hitaansh prompt**  
Its hierarchy says, essentially:  
the source problem alone defines which skills the case exercises.  
The textbook sections establish what students know, but the source problem is the strict skill boundary.  
**My assessment**  
I prefer a hybrid.  
The source question should constrain the **mathematical operation and difficulty**, but primary.md should remain the authority for **pedagogical intent**.  
For example, a source problem might computationally ask students to solve something, while the chapter objective emphasizes interpreting why the result matters. A prompt governed only by the source problem could underemphasize that conceptual objective.  
Our current separation handles this better.  
  
**4. Hitaansh has a much stronger formal scope fence**  
This is one area where the other prompt is clearly stronger.  
It explicitly requires that students be able to solve the case using only:  
    1    concepts supported by the provided sections; and  
    2    information explicitly printed on the worksheet.  
Anything outside those boundaries is banned. Missing concepts from the mapping must be supplied as scenario-level reference information.  
Our prompt says:  
Do not introduce unsupported methods.  
That is correct but much less operational.  
**The advantage of Hitaansh's approach**  
Suppose the source problem requires Skill A, but the generated scenario accidentally requires students to know an additional convention or formula.  
Our prompt tells the model not to do that.  
The Hitaansh prompt gives the model a more explicit procedure for detecting and resolving it.  
**This is one of the strongest parts of the other prompt and worth incorporating in a shorter form.**  
**This is one of the strongest parts of the other prompt and worth incorporating in a shorter form.**  
  
**5. Case construction philosophy**  
Both prompts agree on:  
    •    one scenario;  
    •    connected questions;  
    •    later work building on earlier work;  
    •    compute → interpret → decide;  
    •    clean numbers;  
    •    realistic context;  
    •    a real decision.  
The Hitaansh version is stricter.  
It has named rules including:  
**    •    One Decision, Interlocking Parts**  
**    •    One Decision, Interlocking Parts**  
**    •    Compute, Interpret, Decide**  
**    •    Full-Strength Concepts**  
**    •    Full-Strength Concepts**  
**    •    Minimal Faithful Instance**  
**    •    Minimal Faithful Instance**  
**    •    Context Must Constrain**  
**    •    Context Must Constrain**  
**    •    Honest Verdicts**  
**    •    Honest Verdicts**  
Our prompt contains nearly all of these ideas, but in less formal language.  
**A particularly strong rule from Hitaansh**  
Its **strip test** is excellent:  
mentally remove the story; if the math problem survives essentially unchanged, the context is probably decorative.  
That is a powerful design test because one major problem with generated case studies is that the AI writes:  
“A coffee shop wants to know…”  
and then gives a generic mathematics problem that has no real dependence on the coffee-shop setting.  
Our prompt already says:  
“Do not force a context onto the mathematics.”  
But the strip test is a stronger operational criterion.  
I would add it.  
  
**6. Misconception handling is much more aggressive in Hitaansh**  
**Our current version**  
A misconception should be incorporated **when clearly supported by the input files**.  
A misconception should be incorporated **when clearly supported by the input files**.  
This is deliberately flexible.  
**Hitaansh version**  
A misconception trap is essentially mandatory.  
The model must:  
    1    select a likely misconception;  
    2    create the incorrect calculation;  
    3    ensure it produces a clean, plausible result;  
    4    make the wrong result materially affect or nearly affect the decision;  
    5    often frame it as a teammate's claim.  
**Which approach is better?**  
For struggling students, the idea is excellent.  
But I would **not make it mandatory for every worksheet**.  
But I would **not make it mandatory for every worksheet**.  
Some concepts naturally produce a useful misconception comparison. Others do not. Requiring a dramatic “wrong answer that nearly flips the business decision” every time can cause the model to construct artificial scenarios around the trap instead of around the learning objective.  
So I prefer our rule:  
use a misconception when meaningful and supported.  
But I would strengthen the implementation requirements when one is used.  
  
**7. The other prompt has stronger “full-strength concept” protection**  
This is another feature worth borrowing.  
The Hitaansh prompt explicitly distinguishes:  
    •    a trivial or degenerate form of the concept;  
    •    the full-strength form that demonstrates why the concept exists.  
Our prompt indirectly handles this through the difficulty requirements:  
    •    no one-step substitution;  
    •    at least two multistep questions;  
    •    15–20 minute target;  
    •    interpretation and decision requirements.  
But those do not completely guarantee that the **central mathematical concept itself** is being used meaningfully.  
But those do not completely guarantee that the **central mathematical concept itself** is being used meaningfully.  
For example, a case could technically take 15 minutes because of several calculations while still using the core concept in a shallow way.  
A compact addition to ours could say:  
Use the central concept in a meaningful, non-degenerate form comparable in depth to the source problem. Do not simplify away the feature that makes the concept worth learning.  
That would improve the prompt without importing the full P1–P8 process.  
  
