**10. Method concealment**  
This is a major difference.  
**Hitaansh**  
The student-facing worksheet cannot reveal the technique name. Students must recognize which tool to use. Even hints cannot effectively reveal the method.  
**Ours**  
Our version is less strict.  
It focuses on the case and title but does not impose a universal prohibition against naming the technique.  
**My judgment**  
I would be careful here.  
For some instructional settings, method recognition is part of the learning objective.  
For others, especially students struggling with new material, the teacher may want the method explicitly named because the goal is guided practice.  
Therefore I would **not adopt strict universal method concealment**.  
A better general rule is:  
Do not reveal the full solution procedure in the prompt wording. Name the concept or method only when doing so is consistent with the instructional intent in primary.md.  
That keeps the prompt more pedagogically adaptable.  
  
